Advanced Parkinson's Disease
Palliative & End of Life
Refractory resting tremor, Profound bradykinesia, Severe postural instability/falls

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-007
Reason for Visit: Acute "off" state crisis with severe lead-pipe rigidity and a fall leading to total immobility on the floor.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed the patient experienced a complete freezing of gait resulting in an unpredictable fall. The patient is now entirely trapped on the floor, exhibiting severe, intractable axial rigidity and profound bradykinesia, rendering the elderly spouse physically unable to lift them. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to evaluate for occult fractures, manage the severe physical distress, and administer rescue dopaminergic therapy to restore basic mobility prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Spouse states: "He just froze in the hallway and went down like a tree. He is completely stiff, shaking terribly, and I can't move him an inch."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a long-standing history of a severe, unrelenting resting tremor strictly refractory to maximum optimized dopaminergic therapy. There is profound bradykinesia resulting in severe generalized immobility and frequent freezing of gait. Today, the freezing caused severe, intractable hesitancy when attempting to explicitly initiate movement, resulting in a fall directly secondary to profound postural instability. The patient is currently experiencing severe, intractable axial and appendicular lead-pipe rigidity. The spouse reports advanced, severe dysphagia, noting the patient often chokes on water. Micrographia has definitively progressed to an absolute mechanical inability to write, and the patient exhibits hypomimia (a permanently fixed, mask-like facial expression). Advanced dementia and severe visual hallucinations have been noted in the evenings over the past month.
Functional Status: Currently immobile on the floor; normally requires a four-wheeled walker and physical assistance.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme fall risk; caregiver fatigue and physical inability to safely manage floor recoveries.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg (supine) | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient lies rigid on the floor, conscious, hypomimic facies, displaying severe bilateral pill-rolling tremor.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals severe, intractable axial and appendicular lead-pipe and cogwheel rigidity in all limbs. No obvious foreshortening or external rotation of the lower limbs to suggest hip fracture; palpation of the spine, pelvis, and long bones elicits no focal bone tenderness. Neurological exam reveals profound bradykinesia with virtually no spontaneous movement. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by observing the refractory resting tremor, profound bradykinesia (inability to assist with lifting), and the mechanism of injury (severe postural instability/falls).
Environmental Observations: Hallway is narrow, making mechanical lifting difficult. Poor lighting in the corridor.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient missed their midday dose of levodopa/carbidopa due to a prolonged nap and subsequent dysphagia upon waking.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced Parkinson's Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute "Off" State / Freezing Crisis)
Differentials:

Neck of femur fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of limb shortening, absence of external rotation, and no localized pain on passive internal/external rotation of the hips.

Acute ischemic stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilateral, symmetrical nature of the rigidity and tremor, which is entirely consistent with the patient's known Parkinsonian baseline when unmedicated.

Subdural hematoma - Ruled out/unlikely as the spouse witnessed a controlled, sliding fall without any head strike or loss of consciousness, and pupils are equal and reactive.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a profound, acute hypodopaminergic "off" state, resulting in complete immobility, severe lead-pipe rigidity, and a resultant fall. The objective physical exam reveals a rigid, trapped patient with a severe tremor but no clinical signs of acute skeletal trauma. Because the patient is physically immobilized on the floor and the elderly caregiver cannot safely assist them, delaying medical assessment until standard operating hours would result in prolonged, dangerous hypothermia, severe pressure injury development, and profound distress. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely required to clear the patient of acute traumatic injury, safely assist with lifting, and administer rapid-acting subcutaneous rescue medication to reverse the rigid immobility.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough trauma survey clearing the patient of acute fractures. Administered stat subcutaneous apomorphine via the patient's existing PRN rescue pen to rapidly reverse the severe bradykinesia and rigidity. Assisted the spouse and patient in a safe, multi-person floor recovery to the bed once medication took effect.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you notice the medication wearing off and your feet feeling "glued" to the floor (trigger), you must immediately sit down in the nearest chair (behavior) to eliminate the risk of falling before a complete freeze occurs (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral for an occupational therapy home safety assessment and provision of a mechanical lifting device (e.g., Elk lifting cushion).
DME/Safety (Optional): Implementation of sensory cueing devices (laser cane) to assist with freezing of gait.
Follow-up: Neurology team review within 7 days to adjust the baseline levodopa regimen.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient experiences another fall with a suspected head strike, develops new severe limb pain preventing weight-bearing, or if visual hallucinations become severely distressing and agitated, the spouse must call 000 immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Subcutaneous apomorphine provides rapid but temporary relief; baseline oral medication adjustments are definitively required.
Clinical Course: The freezing of gait and severe postural instability are signs of advanced disease and will progressively worsen, mandating permanent mobility aids and high-level supervision.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to mobilize if a "freeze" begins; sit immediately. If a fall occurs and the patient cannot get up, do not attempt to drag them; call the non-emergency community lift assist service or 000 if injured.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The missed pill caused the dopamine levels in the brain to crash, resulting in the severe stiffness. The injection under the skin works within minutes to replace that dopamine and unlock the muscles so we can get you up safely.
Actionable Steps: Set strict, loud alarms for all medication doses. Do not delay taking pills, even by 30 minutes, to prevent these sudden "off" crashes.

